
$theme {}

$title { Projective Shift Maps }

$og_description { A class of functions I think are neat }

$css {
    .render-container {
        height: 75vh;
    }
}

article {

hgroup {
    h1 { Notes on Projective-Shift Maps }

    a class of functions I think are neat :)
}
hr {}


#explorer.dynamic[style=margin-top:1em;border-radius:0.25em;] {

$layout/loadwall[gpu/proj_shift|render-container] { Load interactive renderer? (designed for desktop) }

}


noscript.info-box {
    It looks like you have scripts turned off. Just so you know, there's an interactive WebGPU-based renderer you're missing out on, and a few other interactive visualizations throughout the article where you'll see a handful of screenshots instead.
}

section {

    h2 { Projective Shift Map }

    div { $math/math {
        manual block
        ident z r cos theta:~ phi:~ psi:~ c n i e
        text where:~
        op ( ) + - =
        in

        z_{n+1} = (r + cos(theta-phi))e^{i theta psi} - c
        where z_n = r e^{i theta}
    } }

    i{click and drag} to move around, i{scroll} to zoom in and out; i{press F} to go
    fullscreen

    this visualization is treating each pixel coordinate as a starting value z_0 in the
    complex plane, and running it through the iterative equation above many times, keeping
    track of where it ends up. points that exceed the "escape distance" from the origin are
    grayscale, darker the longer they took to reach that escape threshold. the rest of the
    points are colored according to their final location (as you increase the iterations
    count, you can watch them jump around). green is based on distance from the origin, while
    blue and red are based on the angle around the origin. you can set iterations to zero to
    see what the color mapping looks like prior to any of the points moving.

    i{p.s. you can use the input boxes to exceed the bounds of the sliders}

    i{p.p.s. you can save the canvas as an image in the right click menu}


see also:
https://link.springer.com/article/10.1140/epjs/s11734-022-00440-w

some figures strikingly similar to what's shown in this paper can be achieved by setting squoosh_x ~ 0.6

}

}

